#heading(numbering: none, outlined: false)[Abstract]

A contended lock decides whose critical section runs next. Patel et al. showed that even a lock that grants turns in FIFO order lets threads with longer critical sections take more of the lock, subverting the CPU scheduler. Usage-fair locks fix the allocation but pay for it, and we argue the price has two parts. The first is _migration_: the thread a lock serves also runs its critical section, so every fair hand-off moves the protected data to a new core. The second is _absence_: a lock can choose only among requests that are waiting when it decides, so a thread that is owed service but is still waking up must lose its turn, have the lock held idle for it, or be kept awake by spinning. Delegation locks remove the first cost, because a combiner runs every request where the data already is; no lock removes the second. We present FC-PQ, a flat-combining lock whose combiner serves the pending request with the least accumulated critical-section time, charged to the requesting thread. Inside redb's single-writer path at a fixed 3.0 GHz clock, FC-PQ raises service-time Jain fairness between 1-record and 64-record writers from 0.85--0.86 under MCS to 0.94--0.995, runs 1.17--1.34× as many transactions per second, and incurs 10--18 cross-core cache transfers per transaction where MCS incurs 93--126. It ties u-SCL, which reserves the lock instead, because redb's writers resubmit at once and are never absent. Making FC-PQ's waiters block exposes absence directly: with identical transactions, blocking reaches u-SCL's CPU time at spinning throughput, but with mixed sizes short-transaction clients wake too late and fairness falls as low as 0.65.
